<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bhagvatsinhji</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1865–1944</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Bhagvatsinhji of Gondal</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bhagvatsinhji was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> of Gondal, a princely state in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathiawar">Kathiawar</a>, until his death in 1944. Trained as a doctor at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a>, he was among the most progressive Indian princes: he built railways, roads and schools, made education compulsory for girls, abolished many taxes and sponsored the great Gujarati encyclopedia <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhagavadgomandal">Bhagavadgomandal</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bhagvatsinhji.jpg" alt="Bhagvatsinhji" width="320" height="575" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Photographs from the Lafayette Studio Archive of the V&amp;amp;A, London · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:H.H._Maharaja_Thakore_Shri_Sir_Bhagwant_Singhji_Sagramji_Sahib_Bahadur,_Maharaja_of_Gondal,_GCSI,_GCIE,_1911.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
